Chapter 1. Introduction
Psoriasis as a Whole-Body Network Disease: Reframing Severe Erythrodermic Psoriasis Through the Lens of Fundamental Medicine
1.1 Background
Psoriasis has traditionally been classified as a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory skin disease characterized by hyperproliferation of keratinocytes, aberrant immune activation, and recurrent inflammatory lesions involving the skin and joints. Over the past three decades, advances in molecular immunology have substantially expanded our understanding of psoriasis, identifying critical pathogenic pathways involving the IL-23/IL-17 axis, TNF-α signaling, dendritic cell activation, T-cell dysregulation, and epidermal barrier dysfunction.
Despite these advances, psoriasis remains one of the most clinically heterogeneous chronic diseases encountered in modern medicine. Patients with apparently similar skin manifestations often exhibit markedly different trajectories of disease progression, treatment response, systemic complications, and long-term outcomes. Such observations suggest that psoriasis cannot be adequately understood as a disease confined to the skin, nor can it be fully explained by isolated immune abnormalities alone.
Accumulating evidence now demonstrates that psoriasis is associated with a broad spectrum of systemic abnormalities, including metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes mellitus, obesity, cardiovascular disease, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, chronic kidney disease, neuropsychiatric disorders, sleep disturbances, gastrointestinal dysfunction, alterations of the gut microbiome, and accelerated biological aging. These observations support the concept that psoriasis represents a complex systems-level disorder involving multiple interconnected biological networks rather than a single-organ disease.
The limitations of reductionist disease models become particularly evident in patients with severe erythrodermic psoriasis, where extensive skin inflammation is frequently accompanied by profound constitutional symptoms, severe fatigue, muscle weakness, edema, malnutrition, cognitive impairment, recurrent infections, hepatic dysfunction, and loss of functional independence. In such cases, the severity of systemic manifestations often exceeds what can be explained by skin pathology alone.
Consequently, a broader framework is required to understand the biological complexity underlying advanced disease states.

1.2 From Organ-Based Medicine to Network Medicine
The emergence of systems biology, network physiology, multi-omics technologies, and complexity science has fundamentally transformed our understanding of human disease. Increasingly, chronic illnesses are recognized as disturbances of interconnected biological networks operating across multiple organizational levels, including genes, proteins, cells, tissues, organs, microbial ecosystems, and host-environment interactions.
Within this framework, disease is not viewed as the consequence of a single defective pathway but rather as the result of progressive dysregulation across interacting biological networks. The clinical phenotype observed in patients therefore represents the emergent manifestation of system-wide disturbances rather than the isolated failure of a single organ.
Network Medicine has provided important insights into the interconnected nature of chronic diseases. However, current network-based approaches remain largely centered on molecular interactions and often fail to fully integrate dynamic physiological, ecological, energetic, and structural dimensions of human biology.
To address these limitations, Fundamental Medicine proposes an expanded biological framework that conceptualizes human health as the dynamic equilibrium of interconnected living biological networks operating across multiple hierarchical scales.

1.3 The Fundamental Medicine Framework
Fundamental Medicine is founded upon the principle that health emerges from the harmonious interaction of biological networks responsible for energy generation, biological regulation, and structural integrity.
This framework is organized into a hierarchical architecture consisting of:
Three Foundational Keys


Metabolic Energy
