12.1 Introduction
The therapeutic objective of Fundamental Medicine extends beyond symptomatic improvement.
While reduction of erythema, scaling, pruritus, and inflammatory activity remain clinically important outcomes, these manifestations are considered downstream expressions of deeper biological processes.
Accordingly, successful treatment should not be evaluated solely through changes in skin appearance.
Rather, therapeutic success should be assessed according to the degree to which pathological biological networks are replaced by adaptive biological networks.
The present chapter evaluates the intermediate recovery phase of the patient and examines whether the network restoration strategy described in Chapter 11 successfully induced biological reorganization across multiple physiological systems.
12.2 Transition from Biological Stabilization to Biological Remodeling
During the first phase of treatment, the primary objective was stabilization of biological networks.
This included:
reduction of inflammatory burden
restoration of bowel function
improvement of sleep
improvement of appetite
recovery of mitochondrial energy production
Once these foundational processes became stabilized, the organism entered a second phase characterized by active network remodeling.
Network remodeling refers to:
the process through which previously pathological biological networks are progressively replaced by adaptive and resilient biological networks.
This transition represents one of the most important concepts within the TSPI framework.
The goal is not merely to suppress disease activity.
The goal is to rebuild biological architecture.
12.3 Recovery of the Microbiome Ecosystem Network
