17.1 Introduction
Throughout the history of medicine, several major theoretical frameworks have fundamentally transformed our understanding of human health and disease.
In the nineteenth century, Claude Bernard introduced the concept of the internal environment (milieu intérieur), establishing the foundation for physiological regulation.
In the twentieth century, Walter Cannon expanded these ideas into the theory of Homeostasis, describing the mechanisms through which biological systems maintain internal stability despite environmental change.
Hans Selye subsequently introduced the General Adaptation Syndrome, emphasizing the biological consequences of chronic stress.
Later, Sterling and Eyer proposed the concept of Allostasis, highlighting the adaptive processes through which organisms achieve stability through change.
More recently, advances in systems biology and network science have given rise to Network Medicine, which conceptualizes disease as perturbations within interconnected molecular networks.
Each of these frameworks contributed significantly to the evolution of biomedical science.
However, several important questions remain unresolved.
Specifically:
Why do chronic diseases frequently persist despite suppression of pathological pathways?
Why do different diseases often share common biological disturbances?
Why does restoration of health frequently involve improvements across multiple systems simultaneously?
Why do biological systems sometimes recover in ways that cannot be predicted from individual molecular targets?
The present case suggests that answering these questions requires a conceptual framework that extends beyond homeostasis, allostasis, and network perturbation.
This chapter proposes such a framework.
It is referred to as:
The Theory of Network Harmonization

